  "title": "Star of the County Down",
  "composer": "Traditional (Irish)",
  "year": 1893,
  "origin": "Ulster ballad sung to the traditional air 'Dives and Lazarus' / 'Gilderoy', better known as the hymn tune Kingsfold",
  "tags": ["irish", "celtic", "folk", "ballad", "modal", "dorian", "verse-chorus", "traditional"],
  "styleHints": ["folk", "rock", "pop", "altrock"],
  "instrumentAffinity": ["guitar", "voice"],
  "pd": {
    "basis": "traditional",
    "detail": "The air is the anonymous traditional tune 'Dives and Lazarus' (also 'Gilderoy'), collected from oral tradition and printed in Broadwood and Fuller Maitland, 'English County Songs' (London, 1893), later harmonised as the hymn tune Kingsfold.",
    "sourceEdition": "Broadwood, Lucy E. and Fuller Maitland, J. A., 'English County Songs' (London, 1893), the 'Dives and Lazarus' air; encoded in E minor.",
    "notes": "Melody and harmony only, no lyrics. The 'Star of the County Down' words are credited to Cathal McGarvey (died 1927) and are not carried here. The tune is modal (natural / Dorian minor): the seventh degree is a natural D, not a raised leading tone, which is correct and not an encoding slip."
  },
  "key": "Em",
  "mode": "minor",
  "meter": "4/4",
  "tempoBPM": 104,
  "tempoRange": [80, 132],
  "feel": "straight",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "notes": "Common time throughout. Two bars per sung line: the verse is one four-bar couplet plus its answer (8 bars), the chorus the same shape lifted an octave at the top. The anacrusis that opens the verse sits in the last beat of the intro; the rising pickup into the chorus sits at the end of the verse.",
